@node gpui-ios User Guide
@section User Guide

This crate is what makes the iOS build of SotF a real iOS app rather
than a Catalyst port. End users do not interact with it directly;
they just see native touch gestures, scrolling and a software keyboard
that behaves the way iOS users expect.

@node gpui-ios Developer Guide
@section Developer Guide

@subheading Architecture

@example
Swift AppDelegate
  |-- sotf_ios_start()       Rust FFI: registers app callback + run_app
  |-- CADisplayLink          Drives gpui_ios_request_frame() per frame
  |-- UIKit lifecycle        Forwarded to Rust via FFI

Rust (staticlib)
  |-- set_app_callback()     Register GPUI app setup
  |-- run_app()              Init GPUI platform, start event loop
  |-- IosPlatform            Implements gpui::Platform for iOS
  |-- IosWindow              Metal rendering, touch dispatch, safe areas
@end example

@subheading Porting a GPUI app

@enumerate
@item Create a Rust @code{staticlib} crate that depends on
  @code{gpui-ios}.
@item Register your GPUI setup function via @code{set_app_callback}.
@item Call @code{run_app()} from Swift through the
  @code{sotf_ios_start()} FFI entry point.
@item In Swift, drive @code{gpui_ios_request_frame()} from a
  @code{CADisplayLink}.
@item Forward UIKit touch events into Rust via the documented FFI.
@end enumerate

@subheading Key FFI symbols

@table @code
@item sotf_ios_start
Entry from Swift; registers the app callback and runs the event loop.
@item gpui_ios_request_frame
Pumps one render frame.
@item gpui_ios_dispatch_touch
Forwards a UIKit touch event into GPUI's input system.
@end table

@subheading Caveats

@itemize @bullet
@item Metal-only; no GL fallback.
@item Safe-area insets are propagated from UIKit, not assumed.
@item The static library must be force-loaded
  (@code{-force_load}) so all platform symbols are kept by the linker.
@end itemize
